1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,300
DMOS, from the inside: what it is, what it does differently, and what it still lacks.

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:11,600
“Just another Debian with KDE?” Fair question. The base is exactly that: Debian 13 and KDE Plasma 6, with no extra repositories or patched packages. DMOS adds identity, Portuguese out of the box, and a first day without the terminal.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,900
1. Identity. It starts at the boot menu: a custom GRUB theme, with Portuguese and English entries and a safe graphics mode.

4
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:25,200
The tide animation: no scrolling text at boot, just the logo over the waves.

5
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:31,500
The login screen, designed from scratch for DMOS.

6
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:39,800
The “Maré” (Tide) desktop: floating top bar and dock, glass effect, a wallpaper generated by code, Papirus icons and the Inter font.

7
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:47,100
Two complete themes, dark and light. One click changes everything: windows, panels, terminal and wallpaper.

8
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:55,400
2. First day. A welcome wizard greets you: pick the look, check the internet, choose news sources, install apps and learn three shortcuts.

9
00:00:55,800 --> 00:01:03,700
Popular apps one click away: Spotify, Visual Studio Code, Steam, Discord, GIMP, OBS Studio, Telegram and Google Chrome.

10
00:01:04,100 --> 00:01:12,000
Flathub is enabled out of the box. On plain Debian you enable it in the terminal; here Discover shows the apps on first boot.

11
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:20,300
DMOS Hub: a desktop panel with the time, a greeting and news. No source is enabled by default, and only the ones you enable are contacted.

12
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:27,600
Ten news sources, Portuguese and international. Your choice, and you can change it anytime.

13
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:36,900
3. Ready to use. Firefox, LibreOffice, VLC and the KDE apps; Wi-Fi firmware for Intel, Realtek, Atheros, Broadcom and MediaTek; virtual machine tools; Portuguese locale, keyboard and time zone preconfigured.

14
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:44,200
Everything at hand in the application menu, with favourites and search.

15
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:52,500
Try it without installing: the ISO boots into a live session. To keep it, the Calamares installer needs about 7 GB.

16
00:01:52,900 --> 00:02:02,800
Frankly, what DMOS is not yet: it is a new, one-person project. Tested on VMware, Hyper-V and QEMU, on a 2017 HP Omen and a 2011 Toshiba Tecra. Secure Boot and dual boot are untested; VirtualBox is slow. Updates come straight from Debian.

17
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:11,100
Try it: free and open source. Download the 2.8 GB ISO at dmos.cc and run it inside Windows with Hyper-V or VMware, or from a USB stick. The tutorials are in the “Comece aqui” playlist.

18
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:18,400
DMOS: a Linux with its own tide. Questions? Ask in GitHub Discussions. dmos.cc
